## Q4, can a filer score stand in for the NVD

{% set q4 = figure("backlog.q4") %}
{{ q4.subtitle }}.

On {{ v("backlog.q4.cves") }} CVEs scored with CVSS 3.1 by both their filer and the NVD, the two agree
well on the attack vector (kappa {{ v("backlog.q4.kappa_av") }}, {{ v("backlog.q4.kappa_av_low") }} to
{{ v("backlog.q4.kappa_av_high") }}) and poorly on attack complexity (kappa
{{ v("backlog.q4.kappa_ac") }}). On the severity band the weighted kappa is
{{ v("backlog.q4.kappa_band") }}, and filers sit lower than the NVD on average
({{ v("backlog.q4.direction_band") }} on the band). A filer score is a usable stand in for the vector's
reach and a weak one for its difficulty and its impact.

{{ table("backlog.q4.by_filer_type") }}

The data lead would push back: a filer has reasons to score its own product low, and the NVD's
score is itself a label made by people. The agreement is between two labels, never with a truth.

## Q5, exploited, and when

{% set q5 = figure("backlog.q5") %}
{{ q5.subtitle }}.

Of {{ v("backlog.q5.cves") }} KEV listed CVEs published since 2021, half were listed within
{{ v("backlog.q5.median_days_to_listing") }} of publication ({{ v("backlog.q5.median_days_to_listing_low") }}
to {{ v("backlog.q5.median_days_to_listing_high") }}), {{ v("backlog.q5.share_listed_within_30d") }}
within 30 days. {{ v("backlog.q5.share_no_nvd_analysis_at_listing") }} had no NVD analysis at the
moment CISA listed them, and {{ v("backlog.q5.share_exploit_before_listing") }} had Metasploit or
Nuclei code before the listing. {{ v("backlog.q5.quarantined_q03") }} listings dated before their
CVE's publication are held back by quarantine rule Q03.

{{ table("backlog.q5.by_year") }}

The security lead would push back: KEV is a lower bound on exploitation. A CVE outside KEV is not
a CVE nobody exploits; it is one CISA has not confirmed.

